('h1','2. Short essay: Should the minimum wage be $15 an hour?'),
('p','In the article, David Neumark argues that a $15 minimum wage is not the best way to help low-wage workers. Low wages are mainly caused by technological change and globalization, which have reduced the value of low-skilled work, and a minimum wage cannot change these forces.'),
('p','From the perspective of supply and demand, a minimum wage is a price floor in the labor market. If it is set above the equilibrium wage, the quantity of labor supplied exceeds the quantity demanded, which causes unemployment. Workers who keep their jobs earn more, but the least skilled may lose their jobs or be replaced by more skilled workers. Neumark also points out that the minimum wage is poorly targeted: many minimum-wage earners are teenagers from middle-income families, while most poor families have no workers at all. As a result, only a small part of the higher wages actually reaches poor families.'),
('h2','1. As an economist, would I prefer a minimum wage or an earned income tax credit?'),
('p','I would prefer the earned income tax credit (EITC). It targets low-income families directly, encourages more people to work instead of reducing employment, and is paid for through the tax system, mostly by people with the highest incomes. A minimum wage, in contrast, is paid by businesses in low-wage industries and by their customers, who are often poor themselves, and it risks destroying jobs for the least skilled.'),
('h2','2. As a politician, which platform would be easier to campaign on?'),
('p','A higher minimum wage would be easier to campaign on. It is simple to explain, it seems to cost the government nothing, and voters see an immediate pay raise. Its costs, such as lost jobs and higher prices, are hidden and spread out. A more generous EITC requires higher taxes, which are unpopular, and it is harder for voters to understand.'),
('p','In conclusion, a $15 minimum wage may help some workers, but it is a blunt instrument. Better-targeted policies such as the EITC can help low-income families more effectively while doing less harm to the least-skilled workers.'),
